{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Fear not, Abram, I am a shield to you.</b> In the Yalkut it brings down: because Abram was afraid — perhaps I have already received my reward in this world and I have nothing at all for the world to come — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, 'Fear not, your reward is set aside for the time to come.' This is what it says: 'exceedingly,' as it is written (Psalms 31:20), ״מָה רַב טוּבְךָ אֲשֶׁר צָפַנְתָּ לִּירֵאֶיךָ״ — end quote. And a difficulty arises against this midrash: if so, what is the meaning of what he said, ״מַה תִּתֶּן לִי וְאָנֹכִי הוֹלֵךְ עֲרִירִי״ and so forth — what do children have to do with the reward of the world to come? And one may settle it along the lines of what is brought in the chapter 'There are those who inherit' (Bava Batra 117a): Rabbi Yohanan said, anyone who does not leave a son to inherit him, the Holy One, blessed be He, is full of wrath against him. It is written here (Numbers 27:8) ״וְהַעֲבַרְתֶּם אֶת נַחֲלָתוֹ לְבִתּוֹ״, and it is written there (Zephaniah 1:15) ״יוֹם עֶבְרָה הַיּוֹם הַהוּא״. And the difficulty is: why did he bring a proof from the Writings (Ketuvim) for a matter of Torah? He should have brought a proof from the verse (Deuteronomy 3:26) ״וַיִּתְעַבֵּר ה׳ בִּי לְמַעַנְכֶם״. But he wanted to bring a proof also for the matter itself and not for the expression alone, for Rabbi Yohanan held that anyone who does not leave a son to inherit him is not exempt from Gehenna, which is called ״יוֹם עֶבְרָה הַיּוֹם הַהוּא״. And Rabbi Yohanan follows his own reasoning, for he interpreted the verse (Psalms 55:20) ״אֲשֶׁר אֵין חֲלִיפוֹת לָמוֹ וְלֹא יָרְאוּ אֱלֹהִים״ as referring to one who does not leave a son — for this is surely because he was not a fearer of Elohim, as it is said (Psalms 128:1) ״אַשְׁרֵי כָּל יְרֵא ה׳״ and so forth, and immediately adjacent to it: ״בָּנֶיךָ כִּשְׁתִלֵי זֵיתִים״ and so forth, ״הִנֵּה כִי כֵן יְבֹרַךְ גָּבֶר יְרֵא ה׳״. Therefore Abram said: 'What reward will you give me for the world to come, when I am going childless' — for my judgment is in Gehenna, as stated? And if you say: to give my reward in this world — concerning this it is written ״וַיֹּאמֶר אַבְרָם הֵן לִי לֹא נָתַתָּה זָרַע וְהִנֵּה בֶן בֵּיתִי יוֹרֵשׁ אֹתִי״. And see this entire derash in parashat Pinchas at the verse ״עֲלֵה הַר הָעֲבָרִים״ (Numbers 27:12), for it is a precious derash."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The citation in Bava Batra is given as קיז (117) in the source; the sugya of 'יֵשׁ נוֹחֲלִין' and the statement of Rabbi Yohanan appear at Bava Batra 116a–b in standard editions. The wordplay on עֲבַרְתֶּם / עֶבְרָה / הָעֲבָרִים is preserved throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:24.976713580Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}